WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:08.270
- So Ayesha Ramachandran
is Associate Professor

00:00:08.270 --> 00:00:09.579
of Comparative Literature.

00:00:09.579 --> 00:00:12.020
Professor Ramachandran
is a literary critic

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:14.930
and cultural historian
of early modern Europe.

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:17.560
Her recent work focuses
on Europe's relations

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:18.960
with an expanding world.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:20.337
And her first book on that topic,

00:00:20.337 --> 00:00:23.640
"The World Makers" was awarded
the MLA Scaglione prize

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.240
in Comparative Literary Studies.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:27.600
The Milton Society of
America's Shawcross prize

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.280
for the best book chapter on Milton

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.320
and the 16th Century Studies Associations

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.470
founder's prize for the
best first book manuscript.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:35.850
In addition to literary and intellectual

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:38.240
historical questions, she is interested

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:41.310
in early modern maps,
particularly world mapping,

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:44.920
the history of science and
technology, early modern empires,

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:47.790
and the rich visual archive
of illustrated books

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:48.770
in the period.

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:50.167
Her current book project

00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:53.350
"Lyric Thinking: Humanism
Selfhood Modernity"

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:55.330
argues for the central importance

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:58.470
of lyric form and language
in shaping new intellectual

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:00.070
possibilities for the self

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:02.640
in the early modern period and beyond.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.382
Christophe Schuwey is
assistant professor of French.

00:01:05.382 --> 00:01:07.030
Cause I'm gonna apologize in advance

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:09.450
I took French for reading
and so I can't speak French.

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:11.020
So I'm gonna blunder
a few of these things.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:14.160
But Professor Schuwey specializes

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.860
in the literature and culture
of 17th century France.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:18.960
His research focuses on the book market,

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.430
fashion and marketing
in the classical age,

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:22.930
the circulation of news,

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:25.560
the making of cultural hierarchies
and cultural transfers,

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.430
especially between England and France.

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:30.147
He's the author of and here
I'll read the translation only,

00:01:30.147 --> 00:01:32.627
"Painting the Times Through
Comedy: an introduction to

00:01:32.627 --> 00:01:34.910
"Molière's "Misanthrope" and "Tartuffe""

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:37.420
and coauthor of three critical editions,

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:40.070
the Nouvelles Nouvelles by Donneau de Visé

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:43.560
Le Parnasse réformé by Gabriel Guéret

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:47.150
and a forthcoming edition
of the Alcoran de Louis XIV.

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:49.750
Professor Schuwey is thus also active

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:51.090
in the field of digital humanities,

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:52.440
which we'll hear more about today.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:53.830
With the mentioned critical editions,

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:56.360
multiple databases including

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:59.200
the birth of dramatic criticism
in 17th century France.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:00.830
Again, I'm reading the translation

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:05.057
and "Book Interfaces:
What Digital Humanities

00:02:05.057 --> 00:02:06.640
"can do for Literature."

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:10.518
Please welcome professors
Ramachandra and Schuwey.

00:02:10.518 --> 00:02:13.390
(crowd clapping)

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:14.653
- Oh, perfect. Okay.

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:16.343
Take it away.

00:02:16.343 --> 00:02:19.151
- Yeah. Can we put away our mask?

00:02:19.151 --> 00:02:22.330
Yep. Thank you. Thank you.

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:25.330
Well, hello everyone.
We're very glad to be here.

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:30.150
Ayesha and I, to talk a bit
about what is available to you

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.240
in terms of resources and
help about this thing,

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.353
that is terrifying thing that
is called digital humanities.

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:40.400
There has been a lot of
debates around institution

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.580
on what is digital humanities
as always with any fields.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:45.490
There are questions of power.

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:49.035
There are question of resources
that are at play here.

00:02:49.035 --> 00:02:52.261
So there are multiple definitions
of digital humanities.

00:02:52.261 --> 00:02:55.650
And one definition that
could be representative

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:59.810
of what we are doing at Yale
is maybe the simplest one,

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:01.420
the uses of computers to study,

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:04.033
contribute to and discuss the humanities.

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:07.150
So what do we mean by that?

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:09.430
Maybe the best one, the
best way to address that

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:11.950
is to talk a bit about our own stories

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:14.150
and our relationship to
the digital humanities.

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:18.820
So I came to digital humanities
because during my PhD,

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:22.640
I start using old 17th
century French books

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:24.740
that were not available in my hometown,

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:27.080
which is Switzerland, Fribourg

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:30.230
but that was only available in Paris.

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:33.570
So it was easier for
me to use Google Books,

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:35.880
to access them then to
go to Paris every week

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:37.870
to check out these books. Right?

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:40.280
So I started to use
digital versions of books,

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:43.220
and then I started to use plain
text search in Google Books

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:46.170
to find things that was in
it that I was interested in.

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:48.520
And then I use library catalogs,

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:51.470
digital library catalogs
to start to find trends.

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:52.590
Is it this word,

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:57.120
a buzz word between 1660
and 1670, for instance.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:02.120
And all this kind of every
day application of computers

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:05.470
to the study of humanities.

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:07.940
Right? So when I arrived in Paris,

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:10.669
I suddenly realized that
there was a whole field

00:04:10.669 --> 00:04:15.180
called digital humanities,
where we were making graphs,

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:16.540
where we were making trees,

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.740
where we were making maps, et cetera.

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:23.640
And I both discovered how useful
this kind of representation

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:25.530
and new approaches to the humanities were,

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:30.530
but also how they can sometimes
take all the resources

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:34.810
and drive us away from the actual work,

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:37.140
which humanities and
what the actual questions

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:42.160
questions we're interested
in, in the humanities.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.409
So this is why I wrote this
book called "Interfaces"

00:04:45.409 --> 00:04:47.790
which is a book that says...

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:50.510
Actually it is as much digital humanities

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:52.720
to use a library catalog

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:56.250
than to make a big data
project that will analyze

00:04:56.250 --> 00:05:00.840
topic modeling across two
century of texts, right?

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:04.640
All of this is using
the computers to change

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:08.610
our relationship and work and questions

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:11.450
to the digital humanities.

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:15.765
Because as useful big data is,

00:05:15.765 --> 00:05:19.870
the transformation that
digital humanities is creating

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:22.200
for the humanities is a
kind of everyday thing.

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:23.880
The fact that we're accessing resources,

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:28.600
that we are writing, not
using a pencil but a computer,

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:30.060
is changing the way we're working.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:31.540
Is changing the way we're thinking.

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:34.050
Is changing the question
we are trying to address.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:36.652
And this is the approach
that kind of minimal,

00:05:36.652 --> 00:05:39.160
but as inclusive as it can be

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:43.623
approaches that we try
to rely on here at Yale.

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:47.533
- That's actually funny.

00:05:47.533 --> 00:05:50.440
I didn't know Christophe's
origin story. So now I do.

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:51.500
And actually it's very funny

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:53.130
because I have a similar origin story.

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:54.069
I was writing my dissertation,

00:05:54.069 --> 00:05:57.920
trying to track the
appearance of the word world

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:00.380
across a body of texts over two centuries

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:01.610
and did the same thing.

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:03.630
I was like, well, this was,
of course in the early 2000s

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:06.931
before the kind of huge
blowing up of big data searches

00:06:06.931 --> 00:06:08.390
and large kinds of...

00:06:08.390 --> 00:06:10.510
It was the early days
of the Google and Gram.

00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:13.780
And I began to think, wow, what
are the databases of texts?

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:15.456
And how do we begin to
actually begin to sort

00:06:15.456 --> 00:06:18.920
and understand empirically if we can track

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:21.390
these set of thematics and concepts

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:23.810
lexically across multiple texts.

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:26.400
And I did some of that
work for my dissertation

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:28.130
and then quickly became disillusioned

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:30.710
with the kind of grand possibilities.

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:33.110
It was kind of the early days of DH Steel

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:35.500
and kind of moved away
from the digital humanities

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:37.150
in part, because I asked a question,

00:06:37.150 --> 00:06:39.470
I think, many humanists ask, which is,

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:42.600
do I really need to go to
computers and count things

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:44.710
in order to answer interpretive questions

00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:46.480
that are actually at the
heart of the humanities?

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:48.873
And so, in what ways is digital humanities

00:06:48.873 --> 00:06:51.448
or the promise of databases, big data,

00:06:51.448 --> 00:06:54.550
text mining, spatial analysis, et cetera,

00:06:54.550 --> 00:06:57.580
really just a kind of grand
dream of making the humanities

00:06:57.580 --> 00:06:59.050
more like the sciences,

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:01.760
as opposed to actually
asking deeper methodological

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:04.630
questions about what these tools can give,

00:07:04.630 --> 00:07:06.617
kind of traditional
questions in the humanities

00:07:06.617 --> 00:07:09.440
and support kind of traditional
training in the humanities

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:11.490
which most graduate students
actually still get today

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:13.390
and which is what I
teach in my own classes.

00:07:13.390 --> 00:07:14.541
I found my way coming back,

00:07:14.541 --> 00:07:16.480
interestingly to the digital humanities,

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:19.360
after a long phase of what I would call

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:20.820
a kind of skeptical crisis,

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:22.830
which is having used these methods myself,

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:25.690
I sort of thought, well,
this is really not helpful.

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:27.330
This has just become a thing.

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:29.730
I'm not interested in a kind of scientism

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:32.065
in part because my own work
is in the history of science.

00:07:32.065 --> 00:07:34.530
And then I found myself in my new project,

00:07:34.530 --> 00:07:36.010
which is about lyric poetry

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:38.260
and thinking about global lyric poetry

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:41.573
on a kind of, you know,
scale that is impossible

00:07:41.573 --> 00:07:44.454
to look at bibliographically
by an individual person,

00:07:44.454 --> 00:07:46.270
to think, well, okay.

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:49.094
So do computers actually
have something to tell us

00:07:49.094 --> 00:07:52.580
or help us in synthesizing,
putting together

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:55.520
and thinking differently about
the objects of our study.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:57.100
And one of the things
that's important here,

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:58.240
I think, about this definition,

00:07:58.240 --> 00:07:59.710
which in some way is deeply simple

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:01.910
but also incredibly complicated is,

00:08:01.910 --> 00:08:03.700
computers just think in two ways,

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:07.530
yes or no, zero or one, this
is not how humanists think.

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:10.270
So if you wanna ask the computer
to do something for you,

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:11.840
any kind of analytical work,

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:14.230
you have to re-craft the side of question

00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:17.430
as a humanist you were
asking a computer to do.

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:18.940
And this is true for super computing,

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:20.880
This is true for like
the very basic question

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:23.330
of is this yes or no, on or off.

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:25.000
There's a kind of powerful binary

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:26.430
in the way in which computers work.

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:28.874
And so to re-imagine
bibliographical questions,

00:08:28.874 --> 00:08:30.400
what is a poem?

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:33.220
Does this count as this kind
of poem or that kind of poem?

00:08:33.220 --> 00:08:37.240
Requires you to ask really
profoundly precise questions

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:39.950
about the object of your study,
which often as humanists,

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:41.090
we're not trained to ask.

00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:44.040
So I found my way back to
this project I'm working on

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:46.569
right now, which is a
census of lyric poetry

00:08:46.569 --> 00:08:49.070
for the early modern
period in a global scale,

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:51.680
which is deeply collaborative,
deeply humanistic,

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:55.100
but has also forced me in the
process of just articulating

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:56.640
what is my data model

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:58.750
to ask questions about poetics

00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:01.580
that usually people who
write about poetry don't ask.

00:09:01.580 --> 00:09:05.050
And that has produced a lot of
interesting intellectual work

00:09:05.050 --> 00:09:08.430
almost alongside and
separate from the business

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:09.730
of actually trying to figure out

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:11.750
how would I build a data
model to talk about poems?

00:09:11.750 --> 00:09:15.280
And how do we analyze large
scale volumes of texts

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:17.750
to find poems that we may
not otherwise have catalog?

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:19.820
Right. So my origin story

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:22.818
in some ways is a story of
both fascination skepticism

00:09:22.818 --> 00:09:25.790
and then a kind of
almost reluctant return.

00:09:25.790 --> 00:09:27.730
But a reminder that digital humanities

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:29.600
is not just about
learning a bunch of tools,

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:31.240
which we're gonna tell
you about in a moment.

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:33.210
But also about an encounter

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:35.230
with a different paradigm of thinking.

00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:38.094
And a paradigm of thinking
that can exist alongside

00:09:38.094 --> 00:09:41.070
and in conversation with
the ways in which humanists

00:09:41.070 --> 00:09:42.610
are traditionally trained. Right?

00:09:42.610 --> 00:09:44.560
And I think Yale is in
some ways a great place

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:46.205
to do that kind of conceptual work

00:09:46.205 --> 00:09:49.190
because we're not an
institution that's invested

00:09:49.190 --> 00:09:51.718
in the fantasy of the tool.

00:09:51.718 --> 00:09:55.690
We are an institution that is
deeply embedded in thinking

00:09:55.690 --> 00:09:58.070
for a logically, thinking
in comparatively,

00:09:58.070 --> 00:09:59.900
thinking in all of the
kind of traditional ways

00:09:59.900 --> 00:10:02.780
in which interpretively in
which humanists are trained.

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:04.750
So how do we take that
traditional training

00:10:04.750 --> 00:10:07.517
and think about this new
or so-called new method

00:10:07.517 --> 00:10:09.230
and what can it do for us?

00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:10.220
And I think that this is a place

00:10:10.220 --> 00:10:11.820
with a lot of resources for that.

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:14.479
So we thought we would turn
briefly to just talk about

00:10:14.479 --> 00:10:19.330
some examples of the kinds of
digital human humanities work

00:10:19.330 --> 00:10:22.490
that people are doing in our
fields in the humanities,

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:24.500
and which are similar to Yale.

00:10:24.500 --> 00:10:26.077
So we'll just go back and forth between us

00:10:26.077 --> 00:10:27.190
and start by looking at...

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:29.520
Christophe's has kindly put a map here.

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:31.603
This is for my pleasure. Thank you.

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:35.140
A lot of my work work on mapping,

00:10:35.140 --> 00:10:36.670
the history of mapping in the 16th century

00:10:36.670 --> 00:10:39.030
began actually with looking at maps online

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:40.400
before I actually went to the libraries

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:42.240
that held these books.

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:43.900
And there's, I mean,
there's a lot we can say

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:46.194
about the relationship
between the digital collection

00:10:46.194 --> 00:10:48.340
and the actual object,

00:10:48.340 --> 00:10:50.443
and that's a whole field of study itself.

00:10:50.443 --> 00:10:53.068
But Yale because of its
extraordinary collections

00:10:53.068 --> 00:10:55.331
is a place in which the
interface of digital

00:10:55.331 --> 00:10:59.670
and an actual object is
being thought about deeply

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:01.420
across a number of different collections.

00:11:01.420 --> 00:11:03.507
And that is one place in
which digital humanities

00:11:03.507 --> 00:11:06.450
is powerful, alive, and
kind of being made every day

00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:08.300
in our teaching and research here.

00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:13.300
- Yeah. And I would like,
sorry, to add about that.

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:15.370
As much as we can think
about the relationship

00:11:15.370 --> 00:11:16.550
between the original object

00:11:16.550 --> 00:11:19.000
and a digital version of the object,

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:21.430
the alternative before digital humanities,

00:11:21.430 --> 00:11:24.482
where the critical edition in
its wide standardized paper.

00:11:24.482 --> 00:11:29.482
So accessing those texts,
those maps, those material,

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:34.070
even to a photograph that
is not a perfect replica

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:36.850
of the original object is
already bringing in back

00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:39.290
a materiality that was almost lost

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:41.240
for many, many, many decades before.

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:43.890
And this is, I think
something that we humanists

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:47.230
are perfectly trained to think
about and to explore further

00:11:47.230 --> 00:11:48.757
than just saying it's bad

00:11:48.757 --> 00:11:51.000
or it's the revolution of the world.

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:52.950
It's more complicated than that. Right?

00:11:54.463 --> 00:11:58.100
Another kind of project
that is digitally managed

00:11:58.100 --> 00:11:59.190
is digital edition.

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:03.920
I think the screen here can
be used not only to replicate

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:05.350
again the original object,

00:12:05.350 --> 00:12:09.440
but to try off new visual experiences

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:11.630
and new relationships to our object.

00:12:11.630 --> 00:12:13.840
Trying, for instance, to
rethink the very concept

00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:16.960
of the footnote, which
is still very widely used

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:18.530
in digital editions today.

00:12:18.530 --> 00:12:21.390
A footnote, and in French
it's une note de bas de page.

00:12:21.390 --> 00:12:23.010
So it's related to the page.

00:12:23.010 --> 00:12:24.430
Doesn't make any sense anymore.

00:12:24.430 --> 00:12:26.600
So how can we think
reference in a different way

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:27.630
in a digital world?

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:30.870
How can we bring some sort of
different reading experience

00:12:30.870 --> 00:12:34.910
than the linear one from
the start of the book

00:12:34.910 --> 00:12:38.660
to the end of the book, knowing
that often with many books,

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:40.580
we are actually not
reading them differently.

00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:43.580
This is also something that
digital editions can do

00:12:43.580 --> 00:12:46.800
on the top of making
texts more accessible,

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:49.730
more easily accessible, that can be a read

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:54.130
if we need to hear this
text instead of reading it,

00:12:54.130 --> 00:12:55.683
et cetera, et cetera.

00:12:56.670 --> 00:12:57.503
- So the next one?

00:12:57.503 --> 00:12:58.336
- Yep.

00:12:59.500 --> 00:13:00.643
- All right.

00:13:00.643 --> 00:13:01.987
Do you wanna do this one?.

00:13:01.987 --> 00:13:02.820
- I can start and then...

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:04.000
- Yeah

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:07.690
- But so all this is not to
say that these are most visible

00:13:07.690 --> 00:13:10.350
part of the digital humanities
that are graphs, maps, trees

00:13:10.350 --> 00:13:14.350
to quote from commodities
book is not important. Right?

00:13:14.350 --> 00:13:17.020
But I think it should be
taken into a more general

00:13:17.020 --> 00:13:21.040
broad approach that it's
another way of looking at things

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:26.040
the same way a digital edition
or a photograph of material

00:13:26.150 --> 00:13:28.020
is another way of looking at something.

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:30.300
Here's another way of looking at our text.

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:33.740
And sometimes it doesn't answer
any question that is useful.

00:13:33.740 --> 00:13:36.438
And sometimes it does
answer many questions

00:13:36.438 --> 00:13:37.300
that we need to know,

00:13:37.300 --> 00:13:40.340
and that were very
difficult to answer before

00:13:40.340 --> 00:13:42.250
and both are true.

00:13:42.250 --> 00:13:43.380
- Yeah. And I would just add to that here,

00:13:43.380 --> 00:13:44.560
looking at these charts,

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:46.600
that one of the things we don't talk about

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:47.737
when we talk about digital humanities

00:13:47.737 --> 00:13:50.170
is the question of data literacy, right.

00:13:50.170 --> 00:13:53.260
And what that simply means
is what kinds of objects

00:13:53.260 --> 00:13:55.200
are data for humanists?

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:57.230
And what is the relationship between them?

00:13:57.230 --> 00:14:00.650
and how is that the grounds
of a kind of analysis

00:14:00.650 --> 00:14:03.950
that allows us to be
critically self-reflexive

00:14:03.950 --> 00:14:05.800
about what we are actually doing.

00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:08.470
Right. So having to think
about humanities objects

00:14:08.470 --> 00:14:10.670
in terms of data, and to think about

00:14:10.670 --> 00:14:12.960
what kinds of relationships
we're setting up with them,

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:15.310
which is the kind of conceptual
work of a digital humanities

00:14:15.310 --> 00:14:17.360
that produces things like this, right?

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:19.950
The setting up of a corpus,
the setting up of a database,

00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:22.680
these things seem deeply
ordinary in a certain way,

00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:24.940
but they also require a level of thinking

00:14:24.940 --> 00:14:27.870
that is not the same thing as
like reading a novel, right.

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:30.360
It's thinking about what
do we do with that novel

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:32.610
when we wanna create a kind
of database of characters

00:14:32.610 --> 00:14:34.693
or count certain kinds of relationships.

00:14:35.665 --> 00:14:36.715
You got the next one?

00:14:37.720 --> 00:14:40.900
All right. So the other
thing that's very familiar

00:14:40.900 --> 00:14:44.000
to humanists in digital
humanities is text mining

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:47.020
or sort of the kind of Corpus searching

00:14:47.020 --> 00:14:49.680
that both Christophe and I
did as our origin stories

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:50.605
of digital humanities.

00:14:50.605 --> 00:14:53.320
This is an example from
the early print lab.

00:14:53.320 --> 00:14:57.007
A project at the University
of Washington at St. Louis

00:14:57.007 --> 00:15:02.007
to which is a deeply tagged
corpus of early modern texts

00:15:03.890 --> 00:15:06.820
which can be searched in
various ways using python

00:15:06.820 --> 00:15:10.440
and other kinds of programs
to understand relationships

00:15:10.440 --> 00:15:13.640
between texts, words and
text and so on and so forth.

00:15:13.640 --> 00:15:15.690
This is the kind of deep textual searching

00:15:15.690 --> 00:15:18.920
that a lot of students will
use or should think about using

00:15:18.920 --> 00:15:20.370
in their projects.

00:15:20.370 --> 00:15:22.790
However, the learning curve to get there,

00:15:22.790 --> 00:15:24.240
and the kinds of things
you can do with them

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:25.553
is not in substantial.

00:15:28.763 --> 00:15:30.332
Alright, this is me again. Okay.

00:15:30.332 --> 00:15:33.380
So this is actually an example
of a project that was piloted

00:15:33.380 --> 00:15:35.160
here at the digital
humanities lab at Yale.

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:37.510
It's kind of extraordinary
project for image analysis.

00:15:37.510 --> 00:15:40.380
So one of the things
that is an affordance,

00:15:40.380 --> 00:15:41.750
I think, of the digital humanities now

00:15:41.750 --> 00:15:44.930
is the ability to analyze
very large amounts,

00:15:44.930 --> 00:15:46.905
not just of text, but also of images

00:15:46.905 --> 00:15:49.260
through the use of neural networks.

00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:52.330
And PixPlot is a tool that was built here

00:15:52.330 --> 00:15:55.780
at the digital humanities
lab in order to do that work,

00:15:55.780 --> 00:15:58.310
to identify sort of analyze relationships

00:15:58.310 --> 00:16:00.530
between very large numbers of images.

00:16:00.530 --> 00:16:02.617
And this is something that
is actually used widely

00:16:02.617 --> 00:16:05.170
in the sciences for various reasons.

00:16:05.170 --> 00:16:07.410
I mean, particularly in like biology

00:16:07.410 --> 00:16:10.910
to look at organisms and
sort of shifting chemistry

00:16:10.910 --> 00:16:13.091
from molecules and for electron analysis.

00:16:13.091 --> 00:16:16.600
It's application in the
humanities is very different

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:18.030
because of the kinds of images

00:16:18.030 --> 00:16:20.040
and the questions we wanna
ask of images. Right?

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:23.740
And so PixPlot is one example
of the kind of large scale

00:16:23.740 --> 00:16:25.210
image analysis that can be done.

00:16:25.210 --> 00:16:26.900
And I will say in my own project right now

00:16:26.900 --> 00:16:29.600
with the lyric poetry and
exploring the possibility of...

00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:32.970
We have many, many pictures
of manuscript pagers,

00:16:32.970 --> 00:16:35.860
and because of the shape of a
poem that is so distinctive,

00:16:35.860 --> 00:16:40.010
can we use image analysis to
identify poems in large scale

00:16:40.010 --> 00:16:43.991
manuscript corpora that
are digitized into images

00:16:43.991 --> 00:16:47.560
that are not already OCR and
we can't actually search them

00:16:47.560 --> 00:16:49.410
textually because they're
in handwriting, right?

00:16:49.410 --> 00:16:53.070
So in what ways do text
and image merge. Right.

00:16:53.070 --> 00:16:56.470
For us to think differently
about the nature of our object.

00:16:56.470 --> 00:16:58.220
- And this is a point, for instance,

00:16:58.220 --> 00:17:00.680
where those kind of
connection and resemblance

00:17:00.680 --> 00:17:02.610
that suddenly the computer shows us,

00:17:02.610 --> 00:17:04.481
maybe some of them are not relevant.

00:17:04.481 --> 00:17:05.314
- Yeah

00:17:05.314 --> 00:17:06.980
- But then this is the ability

00:17:06.980 --> 00:17:08.885
of the digital humanities
to raise question.

00:17:08.885 --> 00:17:11.350
If we decide that it's
not relevant, then good.

00:17:11.350 --> 00:17:12.183
Not a problem.

00:17:12.183 --> 00:17:14.990
But sometimes, also being
able to see so much material

00:17:14.990 --> 00:17:19.490
in such a short time span
is also a way to create

00:17:19.490 --> 00:17:22.000
some relationship that we
didn't see, that we missed

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:24.420
that the critic miss until, until now.

00:17:24.420 --> 00:17:25.550
should we go to our next one?

00:17:25.550 --> 00:17:26.500
- Yes. Yeah

00:17:26.500 --> 00:17:27.898
- Something also that is transformative

00:17:27.898 --> 00:17:30.100
is the access to data.

00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:32.247
That's the very definition
of digital humanities.

00:17:32.247 --> 00:17:34.240
But the fact that we have databases,

00:17:34.240 --> 00:17:38.770
themathic databases of,
for instance, performances

00:17:39.630 --> 00:17:42.610
in the past or, Canterbury performances,

00:17:42.610 --> 00:17:44.180
is also a way to access things

00:17:44.180 --> 00:17:47.030
that were available somewhere.

00:17:47.030 --> 00:17:49.685
But that would require a
tremendous amount of work

00:17:49.685 --> 00:17:51.040
to get it together.

00:17:51.040 --> 00:17:53.310
And every time the question change,

00:17:53.310 --> 00:17:55.070
then you had to go again to the archive

00:17:55.070 --> 00:17:57.050
and ask a different question, right?

00:17:57.050 --> 00:17:58.710
And I don't think that any mathematician

00:17:58.710 --> 00:18:01.827
would say that computer
just made things easier.

00:18:01.827 --> 00:18:04.840
I think that when you can
certainly ask question,

00:18:04.840 --> 00:18:06.820
that takes a couple of seconds to answer

00:18:06.820 --> 00:18:10.060
versus months to answer,
then you deeply change

00:18:10.060 --> 00:18:13.508
the way you approach and
work with the humanities.

00:18:13.508 --> 00:18:14.341
We'll do.

00:18:14.341 --> 00:18:15.296
- No. It's good.

00:18:15.296 --> 00:18:17.376
- So very quickly because I
see you're running out of time.

00:18:17.376 --> 00:18:19.620
Another thing that digital
humanities work does

00:18:19.620 --> 00:18:22.170
is large various kinds
of spatial analysis.

00:18:22.170 --> 00:18:23.910
This is the project in classics,

00:18:23.910 --> 00:18:25.840
it's the digital and
Atlas of the Roman empire,

00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:28.320
which is a lot of people use.

00:18:28.320 --> 00:18:31.037
The production, to think about
the relationship of space

00:18:31.037 --> 00:18:33.490
and sort of humanistic objects.

00:18:33.490 --> 00:18:36.690
Again, produces many different
kinds of spatial analysis

00:18:36.690 --> 00:18:39.800
possibilities from the use of
maps or the creation of maps

00:18:39.800 --> 00:18:43.400
to the use of GIS, to
map various kinds of data

00:18:43.400 --> 00:18:46.680
on to kind of various
sorts of spatial locations.

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:48.680
That's the kind of work
that a number of people

00:18:48.680 --> 00:18:50.410
in the humanities are increasingly doing.

00:18:50.410 --> 00:18:53.440
And that again, produce the
ability to ask and answer

00:18:53.440 --> 00:18:54.890
different kinds of questions.

00:18:56.520 --> 00:18:59.216
- So, this circles back to
something that we touched on,

00:18:59.216 --> 00:19:02.860
digital humanities, but
also humanities as data.

00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:05.640
Actually what digital humanities
allows us also to think

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:07.650
is that what we consider as humanities

00:19:07.650 --> 00:19:10.810
is also sometimes data
that can be represented

00:19:10.810 --> 00:19:11.643
in a different way.

00:19:11.643 --> 00:19:14.540
That makes us get away from
the only textual narrative

00:19:14.540 --> 00:19:16.220
parroting to something different.

00:19:16.220 --> 00:19:18.730
Sometimes what we think
is purely humanistic

00:19:18.730 --> 00:19:21.013
is actually data and can
be better represented

00:19:21.013 --> 00:19:24.737
in different form of
data, maps, et cetera,

00:19:24.737 --> 00:19:27.370
Then sometimes narrated in a text.

00:19:27.370 --> 00:19:29.620
So do you want to add something?

00:19:29.620 --> 00:19:34.428
So question for you very more
concrete. What do we have?

00:19:34.428 --> 00:19:36.678
(mumbling)

00:19:37.800 --> 00:19:39.320
What resources do we have at Yale?

00:19:39.320 --> 00:19:41.143
That's the technical issues?

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:46.100
So there is many initiative at
Yale for digital humanities,

00:19:46.100 --> 00:19:47.870
people doing digital humanities at Yale,

00:19:47.870 --> 00:19:49.130
as you will find out.

00:19:49.130 --> 00:19:51.156
But there are two, I
would say main resource

00:19:51.156 --> 00:19:52.890
that you should think about.

00:19:52.890 --> 00:19:54.610
So the first one is the Yale DHLab

00:19:54.610 --> 00:19:55.910
that is in the Sterling Library.

00:19:55.910 --> 00:20:00.750
The Yale DHlab offers support
for any of your project.

00:20:00.750 --> 00:20:02.181
You go there during your office hours,

00:20:02.181 --> 00:20:06.020
whatever your level
is, how precise you are

00:20:06.020 --> 00:20:08.760
or how un-precise you are, you go there,

00:20:08.760 --> 00:20:12.855
you talk with them about your
project and they will help.

00:20:12.855 --> 00:20:15.530
They can offer grants
to start your project.

00:20:15.530 --> 00:20:16.767
They can offer technical support.

00:20:16.767 --> 00:20:20.880
They can offer a wide array of support

00:20:20.880 --> 00:20:23.430
and both technical and
advice and development

00:20:23.430 --> 00:20:24.540
and technology, et cetera.

00:20:24.540 --> 00:20:27.667
And please, if you never
did anything digital

00:20:27.667 --> 00:20:30.780
go there because this
is made exactly for you.

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:32.240
If you already know how to do it,

00:20:32.240 --> 00:20:34.830
then you don't need DHLab, at some point.

00:20:34.830 --> 00:20:36.643
The other one is the more,

00:20:37.480 --> 00:20:40.860
let's say scholarly
theoretical initiative.

00:20:40.860 --> 00:20:44.400
This is the initiative that
Ayesha and I are chairing

00:20:44.400 --> 00:20:48.792
sort of DH@Yale, which is
trying to build a community

00:20:48.792 --> 00:20:51.130
around this question of DH.

00:20:51.130 --> 00:20:53.660
So a place where anyone
can come and discuss,

00:20:53.660 --> 00:20:56.261
find other people who are
interested in digital humanities.

00:20:56.261 --> 00:20:57.799
We have a reading group.

00:20:57.799 --> 00:21:00.330
The first meeting will be November 30.

00:21:00.330 --> 00:21:02.440
If you're interested,
please write to any of us

00:21:02.440 --> 00:21:05.760
to be added to the list

00:21:05.760 --> 00:21:08.270
where we try to understand
where is the field,

00:21:08.270 --> 00:21:09.190
where is the field going?

00:21:09.190 --> 00:21:11.870
What we can do with digital
humanities, find support,

00:21:11.870 --> 00:21:15.200
find project that you might be
interested to collaborate in.

00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:18.400
And so please join whatever your level,

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:22.300
whatever your interest and we
would be happy to have you.

00:21:22.300 --> 00:21:23.230
- I just want to close us out

00:21:23.230 --> 00:21:27.070
by saying that Yale humanities is powerful

00:21:27.070 --> 00:21:29.200
for its connection to its collections,

00:21:29.200 --> 00:21:33.319
its long histories of training
here that go back centuries.

00:21:33.319 --> 00:21:36.790
But it's not the humanities at
Yale are not like at Stanford

00:21:36.790 --> 00:21:41.790
or MIT that have been tied
to the development of tools

00:21:42.201 --> 00:21:45.090
that the technical development in the ways

00:21:45.090 --> 00:21:46.810
that the kind of digital humanities labs

00:21:46.810 --> 00:21:48.290
at those institutions have evolved

00:21:48.290 --> 00:21:50.500
over the last sort of 15 years or so.

00:21:50.500 --> 00:21:53.780
Instead, I think what we do
here, which is very powerful,

00:21:53.780 --> 00:21:56.300
and this is where the
DH@Yale sort of working group

00:21:56.300 --> 00:21:57.633
is a good place to get started

00:21:57.633 --> 00:22:01.510
is to think conceptually
about how the interaction

00:22:01.510 --> 00:22:03.570
or the interface to use Christophe's words

00:22:03.570 --> 00:22:06.290
with computers and the
humanities allows us to think

00:22:06.290 --> 00:22:08.340
differently, ask different questions

00:22:08.340 --> 00:22:11.840
and go to the tools we need
really to answer the questions

00:22:11.840 --> 00:22:14.450
that are generated by our own research.

00:22:14.450 --> 00:22:17.390
So lesser kind of, I
would say fetishization

00:22:17.390 --> 00:22:20.120
of the possibility of
the internet revolution

00:22:20.120 --> 00:22:23.400
and more about thinking
in powerfully new ways

00:22:23.400 --> 00:22:26.200
about the way in which the
binarism of the computer

00:22:26.200 --> 00:22:28.800
and the complexity of
the humanities interact

00:22:28.800 --> 00:22:32.240
to create new models of thinking research,

00:22:32.240 --> 00:22:35.257
scholarship, and teaching. So thanks.

00:22:35.257 --> 00:22:38.007
(crowd clapping)

00:22:40.800 --> 00:22:45.010
So we're happy to take
some questions. Yeah.

00:22:45.010 --> 00:22:46.610
- [Woman] Thank you for
a wonderful presentation.

00:22:46.610 --> 00:22:48.700
I was curious, I've read
your history of science

00:22:48.700 --> 00:22:50.650
piece in there, and I was curious,

00:22:50.650 --> 00:22:54.160
how do you track or how
would we be cognizant

00:22:54.160 --> 00:22:57.140
of when terminology can shift for things?

00:22:57.140 --> 00:22:59.760
So descriptions of conditions or ailments

00:22:59.760 --> 00:23:00.593
or things like that,

00:23:00.593 --> 00:23:04.416
the language around
them evolves over time.

00:23:04.416 --> 00:23:06.470
- So that's actually a great question.

00:23:06.470 --> 00:23:08.290
And then this is where I
think it's also an example

00:23:08.290 --> 00:23:10.730
of how digital humanities is
not about just plugging stuff

00:23:10.730 --> 00:23:12.360
into a computer, right?

00:23:12.360 --> 00:23:14.120
So I think one of the
things that's really tricky

00:23:14.120 --> 00:23:15.360
about that kind of scholarship

00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:18.220
is you have to do a fair
amount of preliminary work

00:23:18.220 --> 00:23:22.080
to be able to know what your
search terms are, at all.

00:23:22.080 --> 00:23:24.950
Right. And so that also means
working collaboratively.

00:23:24.950 --> 00:23:26.730
And this is something that I
think humanists in particular

00:23:26.730 --> 00:23:27.870
are not very well-trained to do

00:23:27.870 --> 00:23:29.530
in a way that scientists really are,

00:23:29.530 --> 00:23:30.960
which is to not think of yourself

00:23:30.960 --> 00:23:32.400
as the generator of knowledge,

00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:34.740
but to think of a distributed model

00:23:34.740 --> 00:23:36.110
in which you might have to collaboratively

00:23:36.110 --> 00:23:37.600
go to a lot of people and be like

00:23:37.600 --> 00:23:40.731
you're 14th century specialists,

00:23:40.731 --> 00:23:43.410
how was this thing talked
about in those texts

00:23:43.410 --> 00:23:46.430
and then generate, you
know, your own corpus

00:23:46.430 --> 00:23:49.260
of search terminology that
then you would be able to feed

00:23:49.260 --> 00:23:50.650
into a number of different databases

00:23:50.650 --> 00:23:52.410
to track perhaps contextually

00:23:52.410 --> 00:23:55.260
then how that particular
word or phrase shifts.

00:23:55.260 --> 00:23:57.730
But that does require, I mean,
this is actually crucial.

00:23:57.730 --> 00:24:00.620
It does require kind of preliminary work

00:24:00.620 --> 00:24:02.930
before you can apply a digital technique.

00:24:02.930 --> 00:24:04.980
And so I think that often the
sense of digital humanities

00:24:04.980 --> 00:24:07.216
is, you know, we're gonna go
and like get something quickly.

00:24:07.216 --> 00:24:10.350
And I think (mumbles and giggles)

00:24:10.350 --> 00:24:13.010
- Exactly the magic. Magic
thinking of digital humanities.

00:24:13.010 --> 00:24:16.040
- And the truth is like,
it takes a very long time

00:24:16.040 --> 00:24:18.610
to even do things like figure
out like what's my corpus.

00:24:18.610 --> 00:24:20.790
I mean, I'm still spending
all this time thinking about

00:24:20.790 --> 00:24:22.600
like, what is a lyric poem

00:24:22.600 --> 00:24:23.840
in a bunch of different traditions?

00:24:23.840 --> 00:24:26.130
Like even that terminological question,

00:24:26.130 --> 00:24:28.755
but also the, like how
do we define the object

00:24:28.755 --> 00:24:31.920
in a way that the computer
can understand, right.

00:24:31.920 --> 00:24:35.220
And that binary thinking is
a huge conceptual challenge,

00:24:35.220 --> 00:24:37.270
right? Like that is the
big work. In some ways.

00:24:37.270 --> 00:24:39.260
The small work is the
actual stuff of plugging

00:24:39.260 --> 00:24:41.210
it in and like hitting a button. Right.

00:24:48.467 --> 00:24:51.082
- [Woman] Hi. Thank you so much (mumbles)

00:24:51.082 --> 00:24:55.999
(background noise drowns out other sounds)

00:25:37.987 --> 00:25:41.530
- So I will just say one thing,

00:25:41.530 --> 00:25:43.180
I think that what you're
putting your finger on

00:25:43.180 --> 00:25:45.750
is a larger pedagogical
and curricular issue

00:25:45.750 --> 00:25:47.070
in doctoral training.

00:25:47.070 --> 00:25:50.190
And I will say that one of the aspects

00:25:50.190 --> 00:25:52.210
of the doctoral report that
was published last year

00:25:52.210 --> 00:25:53.993
by the working group
for the graduate school,

00:25:53.993 --> 00:25:58.030
was that departments need to
think about other pathways

00:25:58.030 --> 00:26:00.040
to build in pathways,
into doctoral programs,

00:26:00.040 --> 00:26:02.560
precisely to recognize things like

00:26:02.560 --> 00:26:04.030
if you have to learn Python,

00:26:04.030 --> 00:26:06.800
learning Python is like
learning Latin in a certain way.

00:26:06.800 --> 00:26:09.801
Right. And so can we,
for instance, find ways

00:26:09.801 --> 00:26:13.690
within doctoral training
to create time for students

00:26:13.690 --> 00:26:16.040
to get the skills they might
need to do the scholarship

00:26:16.040 --> 00:26:17.290
that they wanna do.

00:26:17.290 --> 00:26:21.000
And so that is a hugely
important and big question.

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:23.330
In the long-term, I think
there's a lot of conversation

00:26:23.330 --> 00:26:25.260
about how to do that in
different departments.

00:26:25.260 --> 00:26:28.020
You should talk to your
own advisor and DGS

00:26:28.020 --> 00:26:30.440
about ways that you can actually use that

00:26:30.440 --> 00:26:32.060
to meet requirements in your program

00:26:32.060 --> 00:26:33.941
because departments are
becoming flexible about this.

00:26:33.941 --> 00:26:35.270
The shorter term answer,

00:26:35.270 --> 00:26:36.840
I'm gonna leave that to Christophe

00:26:36.840 --> 00:26:38.365
who has acquired many of these skills.

00:26:38.365 --> 00:26:39.339
(Ayesha laughing)

00:26:39.339 --> 00:26:41.560
- I so agree, and that's
a general discussion,

00:26:41.560 --> 00:26:43.410
even in the humanities,
beyond Yale, right.

00:26:43.410 --> 00:26:46.042
We try to, I mean, also the
question of recognition.

00:26:46.042 --> 00:26:48.650
One thing that I think is
interesting in what you're saying

00:26:48.650 --> 00:26:51.410
is there should be a way
to do digital humanities

00:26:51.410 --> 00:26:53.950
with not a lot of technical skills.

00:26:53.950 --> 00:26:56.090
And this is, I think, where for instance,

00:26:56.090 --> 00:26:58.555
something like DH@Yale can help.

00:26:58.555 --> 00:27:03.555
But what I mean beyond that,
is that the conceptual work

00:27:05.120 --> 00:27:08.500
should bring questions that then someone

00:27:08.500 --> 00:27:10.920
who is specialized can answer.

00:27:10.920 --> 00:27:13.820
And digitally, many of
these shouldn't be at least

00:27:13.820 --> 00:27:17.610
this is what I believe
necessarily tacky. It's great.

00:27:17.610 --> 00:27:19.100
Of course, it's fantastic.

00:27:19.100 --> 00:27:20.340
It allows us to think differently

00:27:20.340 --> 00:27:21.480
it allows us to do a lot of things,

00:27:21.480 --> 00:27:24.180
but there should be a way
to do digital humanities

00:27:24.180 --> 00:27:28.360
without the technical skills necessarily.

00:27:28.360 --> 00:27:30.160
This is also some kind
of support that we have.

00:27:30.160 --> 00:27:31.390
So there would be two paths.

00:27:31.390 --> 00:27:33.600
There is the path where
you do the conceptual work.

00:27:33.600 --> 00:27:35.290
And you leave the technical work

00:27:35.290 --> 00:27:37.510
to someone who is an expert,

00:27:37.510 --> 00:27:42.293
or you put the work in, in
learning it with all that.

00:27:43.634 --> 00:27:45.390
- I guess we might disagree
on this a little bit,

00:27:45.390 --> 00:27:47.020
because I think, I mean, my own feeling,

00:27:47.020 --> 00:27:48.740
and this has come out of my own experience

00:27:48.740 --> 00:27:52.390
is you need to be conversant
at least a little bit

00:27:52.390 --> 00:27:54.660
with some level of like
coding and other things,

00:27:54.660 --> 00:27:56.220
because otherwise you kind of don't know

00:27:56.220 --> 00:27:58.130
what your collaborator,
who is the technical expert

00:27:58.130 --> 00:27:59.609
is doing, right.

00:27:59.609 --> 00:28:03.020
So I think collaboration
absolutely is key.

00:28:03.020 --> 00:28:05.040
And like we, as humanists
have to think differently

00:28:05.040 --> 00:28:07.670
about models of creating
and doing scholarship.

00:28:07.670 --> 00:28:08.560
But on the other hand,

00:28:08.560 --> 00:28:10.670
I also think that there isn't, you know,

00:28:10.670 --> 00:28:13.380
there's no corp out to understanding

00:28:13.380 --> 00:28:15.340
basically how a database works.

00:28:15.340 --> 00:28:17.500
Like, I mean, like that's
actually a complicated thing.

00:28:17.500 --> 00:28:19.930
And like one of the
things I would like to see

00:28:19.930 --> 00:28:22.410
in humanities training is that
we offer classes for students

00:28:22.410 --> 00:28:24.230
who actually like get their feet wet

00:28:24.230 --> 00:28:26.740
because in college they didn't
study statistics or whatever.

00:28:26.740 --> 00:28:29.800
Like you kinda need to
know that stuff. Right.

00:28:29.800 --> 00:28:31.620
- No, I, I completely agree with that.

00:28:31.620 --> 00:28:33.300
It's about not making it frightening,

00:28:33.300 --> 00:28:35.010
but anything that we can acquire.

00:28:35.010 --> 00:28:36.340
And it also helped to rethink

00:28:36.340 --> 00:28:38.040
a lot of things in the humanities.

00:28:41.450 --> 00:28:42.559
- Thank you.

00:28:42.559 --> 00:28:45.309
(crowd clapping)

00:28:46.667 --> 00:28:49.250
(upbeat music)

